# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b6), edited. gaston_doray's first conversation.

~ start
set gaston_doray_met
Narrator: Turpentine and wet linen hang sharp around a young man with dry ochre flecking his sparse beard.
gaston_doray [neutral]: Hold still, your faces still wear the grey rind of the river. Tell me, what colour is the sound of a bell drowning in fog?
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask about his paintings and his past -> paintings
* Ask what he thinks of Wickermoor Village -> village
* Ask if he has heard any strange rumours -> rumour
* [Perception DC 14] Study the corners of his wet canvases -> hare_ok | hare_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ paintings
gaston_doray [smile]: I walked out of the mist three years back with an empty head and a full brush. Faces lie, so I paint what sleeps behind the ribs.
-> menu

~ village
gaston_doray [weary]: Provincial theater. They carve wooden mouths into an old oak and call it holiness, though the red mud beneath its roots has a lovely, greedy sheen.
-> menu

~ rumour
gaston_doray [sly]: Something older than the Mayor's Green Queen scratches in the woods past the lantern-line. When the wind drops, you hear branches growing in the dark like antlers.
-> menu

~ hare_ok
set cult_clue_gaston_doray
Narrator: Deep in the indigo brushwork of every canvas, a hare crowned with bone-white prongs stares from the thicket.
gaston_doray [afraid]: Strange, is it not? My wrist paints that little beast in the brambles before I wake, and I dare not scrape it out.
-> menu

~ hare_fail
Narrator: The thick oil paint gleams in the lantern-light, a dizzying smear of moorland bog and crooked moons.
gaston_doray [neutral]: You squint like a magistrate counting tax. Step back before your breath clouds the varnish.
-> menu
